WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.760
All right, my friends.

00:04.760 --> 00:10.440
So with that, we have learned how to insert new rows to our tables and as well how to update the content

00:10.440 --> 00:12.080
of already existing row.

00:12.120 --> 00:18.080
Now the last thing or command that we can do to the data inside the table is that we can go and remove

00:18.120 --> 00:22.160
rows from our table, and we can do that using the command delete.

00:22.160 --> 00:28.280
So if we use delete SQL going to go and start removing already existing rows inside your table.

00:28.320 --> 00:31.360
Alright, now for the syntax of the delete, it's going to be very simple.

00:31.360 --> 00:34.840
We're going to say delete from and then we're going to write the table name.

00:34.840 --> 00:36.720
And here comes something very important.

00:36.720 --> 00:38.680
We have to add a where condition.

00:38.680 --> 00:39.880
And it's like the update.

00:39.880 --> 00:44.480
If you don't do that if you don't include where condition, what's going to happen, you will end up

00:44.480 --> 00:47.200
deleting all the rows inside the table.

00:47.200 --> 00:48.640
So the syntax is very simple.

00:48.640 --> 00:51.400
Let's go back to SQL in order to delete some data.

00:51.440 --> 00:51.640
Okay.

00:51.680 --> 00:52.920
So now we have the following task.

00:52.920 --> 00:56.600
Delete all customers with an ID greater than five.

00:56.640 --> 01:00.200
So now we have to go and delete all the customers that we recently added.

01:00.200 --> 01:01.200
So how we can do it.

01:01.200 --> 01:01.800
It's very simple.

01:01.800 --> 01:03.800
We're going to say delete from.

01:03.800 --> 01:08.410
So that means I want to delete something from a table and we have to specify the table name.

01:08.410 --> 01:09.410
It can be the customers.

01:09.410 --> 01:10.730
So the syntax is very simple.

01:10.730 --> 01:16.810
Now my friends, this is more risky than updates because if you execute it like this don't do that yet.

01:16.810 --> 01:18.090
Wait what can happen?

01:18.090 --> 01:20.890
All the data of the customers going to be deleted.

01:20.890 --> 01:24.010
So you will get an empty table and we will not do that.

01:24.010 --> 01:26.450
So now we're going to do exactly like the update command.

01:26.450 --> 01:28.290
We're going to specify the where clause.

01:28.290 --> 01:30.930
So it says the ID should be greater than five.

01:30.970 --> 01:34.050
So that means ID higher than five.

01:34.050 --> 01:37.530
So with that we are defining a subset of the data that should be deleted.

01:37.570 --> 01:38.450
Not everything.

01:38.490 --> 01:42.890
And if we check in the updates we have here to do a double check before deleting anything.

01:42.890 --> 01:47.210
So again what we do we select star from table customers.

01:47.210 --> 01:51.930
And we're going to go and copy the where condition in order to test what can it be deleted.

01:51.930 --> 01:55.010
So it's going to be all the customers that is higher than five.

01:55.050 --> 02:00.730
And with that I'm making sure that my delete command is correct, which is from what I see here is correct.

02:00.730 --> 02:02.890
So those five customers should be deleted.

02:02.890 --> 02:05.610
So now let's go and delete those customers.

02:05.650 --> 02:07.570
And now very important to read the message.

02:07.570 --> 02:09.610
It says five rows affected.

02:09.610 --> 02:12.140
So that means five customers got deleted.

02:12.180 --> 02:13.380
And this is better than ten.

02:13.420 --> 02:13.900
Of course.

02:13.900 --> 02:16.820
So let's go and check what customers left.

02:16.820 --> 02:18.580
So we have 12345.

02:18.620 --> 02:20.100
Those are the original customers.

02:20.100 --> 02:21.780
And everything else got deleted.

02:21.780 --> 02:23.220
And with that we have solved the task.

02:23.220 --> 02:26.060
And this is how we can delete data from tables.

02:26.100 --> 02:27.300
Be very careful.

02:27.340 --> 02:29.940
Always test before doing the delete command.

02:29.980 --> 02:30.180
Okay.

02:30.220 --> 02:31.420
So now we have the following task.

02:31.420 --> 02:34.980
And it says delete all data from table persons.

02:34.980 --> 02:38.940
So that means we have to go and drop everything from the table persons.

02:38.940 --> 02:40.500
But we don't want to delete the table.

02:40.500 --> 02:43.500
We just want to delete the data inside the table now.

02:43.540 --> 02:46.500
So now what we're going to do we're going to write delete from.

02:46.540 --> 02:48.500
And now we have to specify the table persons.

02:48.500 --> 02:53.140
And if we execute it what can happen is we're going to go and drop all the data in the persons.

02:53.140 --> 02:56.020
But in SQL we have more interesting commands.

02:56.020 --> 03:00.580
If you want to delete everything from the table persons we have that truncate truncate.

03:00.580 --> 03:02.900
It is exactly like delete from persons.

03:02.900 --> 03:05.100
It's going to go and make the whole table empty.

03:05.100 --> 03:05.500
But why?

03:05.500 --> 03:09.060
I like to use truncate because it is way faster than deletes.

03:09.060 --> 03:13.420
If you have large tables, the delete command is going to be really slow because with the delete there

03:13.460 --> 03:15.880
is like a lot of things happening behind the scenes.

03:15.880 --> 03:21.160
There is like logs and protocols, but if you are using trunk, the database can skip all those extra

03:21.160 --> 03:23.520
stuff and it's going to be very fast.

03:23.520 --> 03:26.520
So if you want to delete all the data from table, you can do it like this.

03:26.520 --> 03:27.800
If it's like small table.

03:27.800 --> 03:32.360
But what I usually do I go and write truncate and then table.

03:32.360 --> 03:33.960
We're going to get the same effect.

03:33.960 --> 03:37.320
And with that I'm saying reset everything, make the table empty.

03:37.320 --> 03:38.800
So let's go and execute it.

03:38.800 --> 03:41.720
And now with that you will not get the number of deleted rows.

03:41.760 --> 03:44.120
And that's why it's truncate is way faster.

03:44.120 --> 03:45.880
It is not protocol or anything.

03:45.880 --> 03:47.200
It's not logging anything.

03:47.240 --> 03:51.000
It just go and delete all the data without any extra steps.

03:51.000 --> 03:54.240
So this is how we can delete all the data from a table.

03:54.280 --> 03:56.400
But the table still exists okay my friend.

03:56.400 --> 04:00.920
So with that you have learned the basics on how to manipulate your data inside a database, the data

04:00.920 --> 04:02.680
manipulation language DML.

04:02.680 --> 04:08.520
And with that I can tell you we have covered the basics of SQL so that we have covered the beginner

04:08.520 --> 04:09.040
level.

04:09.080 --> 04:11.920
Now in the next chapters we will be in the intermediate level.

04:11.920 --> 04:15.880
And the first thing that you're going to learn in the intermediate level, you will learn how to filter

04:15.880 --> 04:20.360
your data and how you can recover many operators that you can use inside the Where clause.

04:20.360 --> 04:21.120
So let's go.
